  "user_request_original": "Design a 36-layer alternating Ta2O5/SiO2 multilayer filter on fused silica substrate with air incidence at 0 degrees unpolarized illumination. Initialize all layer optical thicknesses as quarter-wave at 350 nm center wavelength (Ta2O5 physical thickness = 350/(4*n_Ta2O5), SiO2 physical thickness = 350/(4*n_SiO2)). Apply gradient-based thickness optimization with line-search damping coefficient halved relative to baseline. For optimization iterations 1-5, constrain each layer thickness to remain within ±15 nm of its initial quarter-wave value; release this bound for iterations 6+. Enforce hard bounds 20-350 nm on all final thicknesses. Optimize to maximize average transmittance in 420-700 nm band while minimizing average transmittance in 355-365 nm and 1030-1080 nm bands, and minimizing transmittance at 355 nm. Report final thickness array, spectral transmittance from 300-1200 nm, and band-averaged metrics. Canonical user controls: incidence angles 0 degrees. polarization unpolarized. wavelength intervals 355-355 nm, 355-365 nm, 420-700 nm, 1030-1080 nm. soft objectives: high average transmittance in 420-700 nm; low average transmittance at 355 nm; low average transmittance in 355-365 nm; low average transmittance in 1030-1080 nm. behaviors to suppress or avoid: high transmittance at 355 nm; high transmittance in 355-365 nm; high transmittance in 1030-1080 nm; high loss in 420-700 nm. manufacturing constraints: maximum 36 layers; layer thickness range 20-350 nm.",
  "normalized_request_english": "Design a 36-layer alternating Ta2O5/SiO2 multilayer filter on fused silica with air incidence at 0° unpolarized illumination. Initialize all layers to quarter-wave optical thickness at 350 nm. Apply gradient-based thickness optimization constrained to ±15 nm of initial values for the first five steps, then released, with hard bounds of 20–350 nm throughout. Maximize average transmittance in 420–700 nm while minimizing average transmittance in 355–365 nm, 1030–1080 nm, and at 355 nm. Report optimized thicknesses and spectra across 300–1200 nm.",
